d the fetal liver  which seed all organs in these first  waves  and in organs like the brain which  develops the blood brain barrier  after these embryonic macrophages seed  the brain  they are not replaced and they survive  throughout the life of the animal  other populations however lose their  embryonic macrophages over time  and they are replaced by bone marrow  derived macrophages in the al  such as the in the intestine  it's not really clear yet what the  differences are functionally between  aldol and embryonic derived macrophages  so what do macrophages do in the memory  plan  well in puberty as these ducts grow and  branch throughout the  mammary fat pad the macrophages are  required for this growth  in branching and they sit within the  terminal and buds where they clear away  the apoptotic cells  we know that antigen presentation  negatively regulates  this growth but it's not clear whether  this is  macrophages in the adult  macrophages promote mammary stem cell  activity  and they closely interact with the  epithelium and there are some  persistent embryonic macrophages in the  gland  in reproduction they regulate  alveogenesis during pregnancy  and are really important for the  remodeling that happens  in involution  but we really don't know much about the  heterogeneity  of macrophages in the breast tissue yet  and we don't know by which mechanisms  they carry out these functions  so the reason i'm telling you this is  that early in my phd i was looking for  different types of cells that interact  with the mammary ducts  and one of these was immune cells so  this is a 3d confocal image with the  mammary duct here and immune cells in  yellow  and looking at an optical section you  can see  this immune cell stain within the duct  and looking at a thicker section they  start to take form  as the dendritic shaped cells that are  within the epithelium  looking closer with more specific stains  for each of the epithelial layers  shows that these immune cell