  or dms and  matching the imaging they're mhc2 high  and lysic c low  and so we labeled these ones the stromal  macrophages or sn's  and these ones the ductal macrophages  but to confirm that these were the  associated macrophages  i did facts of whole glands this is the  frequency of the dust microfibers and  also of  fat pads which had cleared epithelium  so they have no ducts left and there are  no  duffel macrophages and when we do an  enrichment for ducks  we get into the enrichment of these  macrophages so we know now  that these macrophages the cd11c  positive cd11 below  are very specific to the dots and  they're not present in the stroma  we also found that they are really high  for cx3cl1  whereas the strangle macrophages are  lower for cs3co1  and this is just a marker of some  macrophage populations  but the reporter mice help us to get a  really clear picture of  where they are and what they look like  this is looking at the outer layer of  the dust  with the myopathial cells labeled with  keratin fiber  and this obscures the ductal macrophage  network  whereas looking at the inside of the  duct this network is  revealed with the cxcco1 and then  removing the outer layer of the  epithelium completely you can see all  these cells so they're really really  frequent across the whole gland  so we look at the nipple area the mid  distal and in lactation  and they have a really similar  distribution across all of these areas  we really wanted to know what the  dynamics of this interaction were  how do the ductal macrophages move and  how do they interact  with the epithelium and to do this i  developed an intravital  imaging technique using a skin flap so  we  expose the mammary gland and we can  image for up to 12 hours  we have access to an entire memory lane  which is really important because  most of it is embedded in fat tissue  which is really hard to image through  and so having access to the entire gland  uh  helps to isolate ducts which don't have  