em into  mammary tissue macrophages  and tumor-associated macrophages which  has this really interesting  phenotype and so  this really picked our interest because  the breast tams  are proteomergenic they're not dependent  and they can proliferate  and so all these features seem really  similar to the ductal macrophages  so we took our rna-seq and then compared  it to rna-seq of the  tumor macrophages and the genes that  were up  in the tumor associated macrophages and  the doctor macrophages correlate  really strongly whereas the genes up in  the stromal macrophages correlate  with the mammary tissue macrophages  and then when we look by flow cytometry  we can see the duster macrophages  here the cd11c positive lysose low  during tumor genesis as the cancer grows  we get enrichment for these cells and  for the more they mirror the morphology  and positioning of the doctor  microphotos in the normal duct  within the cancer  so to summarize the mammary gland has a  population of  unique resident macrophages that monitor  the epithelium  they're bone marrow derived during  puberty but before that  they are embryonic derived they  promote epithelial remodeling by  clearing away the  dyeing milk producing cells during  involution  and they're really similar to breast  tumor macrophages  and we hope that this discovery will  help us to understand  better tumor macrophages  and help us to modulate those to improve  cancer treatment  and so these dr macrophages they have a  unique  markers compared to the strabal  macrophages and they have this  intraepithelial localization  during pregnancy they proliferate to  increase in number  so they cover the whole gland and all of  the alveoli in lactation  and then in involution they infiltrate  the lumen and clear away these  dying milk producing cells and then in  tumor genesis in breast cancer they  maintain this positioning  throughout the cancer  so i'd like to thank now uh especially  my  supervisors jane viveda and rios an