because  it has been implicated in  mammary gland macrophages before and  also stem cell regulation  and to test this we  had a not reporter master this is a  notch target  gene uh with the gfp downstream  and it's only active during  active notch signaling and this was  specifically  on in the adaptable microfibers but not  the other  immune cells  and so to look at whether not signaling  was important we  looked at cv11c cree rbpj  flock slice and this  knocks out rbpj which is a  an effector transcription factor of not  signaling  specifically industrial macrophages  and when we did this it didn't actually  decrease their number significantly  but rather than being cd11b low  they remain cd11b high so we think that  notch might be important for the  maturation of ductal microphotos  and it doesn't really affect their  localization they're still within the  epithelium  to see what they might be doing during  remodeling of the mammary gland  i did facts across all of these time  points through pregnancy  lactation and involution and found that  this  really striking increase in doctor  microphones into involution  where they really dominate the immune  landscape  this uh increase is driven in part by  proliferation of the microphotos within  the epithelium  not neurons coming in  and so to ask why there might be so many  of them here  i did imaging to see where they were and  they're  on the outside of the alveolar in  lactation and these are the milk  producing cells here  but at four days in volusion as the milk  producing cells are dying  and the alveoli collapse the ductile  macrophages infiltrate  into the lumen and they surround the  dying cells  so it looks like they might be vegas for  toasting these and clearing them away  to get a clearer picture of this i  looked a bit earlier before  the alveoli had collapsed  and here the milk producing cells  expressed the gfp in purple  and looking closer  you can see that these milk producing  cells which have a  slightly dimmer gfp because they're  dying  are being engulfed by the doppler  macrophages with the mhc2 signal here  and really interestingly the milk  producing cells are binucleated so they  have these two really distinct  nuclei of per cell and within the  duplicate microphotos you can see these  two nuclei and see that these milk  producing cells are being phagocytes  but is this important are the doctor  microphones necessary for this  remodeling  and to answer this we depleted them  using the diphtheria toxin model  so if you express the seriotoxin  receptor  in specific cells you can administer  diphtheria toxin  and this will bind to this receptor and  kill just those cells  and so we treated cd11c dtr  mice with the serotoxin upon weaning and  then collected them during involution  and in the control mice we have this  infiltration of the dms  and phagocytosis of the dying cells  whereas when we deplete  we have no dms and we have no  phagocytosis  of the dying cells  during evolution the lumen collapse and  the alveoli collapse  to allow remodeling to the normal ducts  and this occurs normally in the controls  but when we deplete the dms we get a  failure of the lumen to collapse and so  this remodeling is really badly affected  and this is really important for  uh later pregnancies  and has implications for  cancer as well  so through this discovery we've really  nailed down a population that is  important for  this remodeling during evolution but  there are a lot of other  areas that macrophages are important for  in mammary gland development that  still need further investigation as to  which populations are important and  what what mechanisms  now switching tact a bit into breast  disease  in breast cancer breast macrophages  or tumor associated macrophages  are different to the normal macrophages  so in the normal breast we have the c11b  positive cells and as tumors progress  they get more and more of these cd11b  low  tams  so this study separated them into  mammary tissue macrophages  and tumor-associated macrophages which  has this really interesting  phenotype and so  this really picked our interest because  the breast tams  are proteomergenic they're not dependent  and they can proliferate  and so all these features seem really  similar to the ductal macrophages  so we took our rna-seq and then compared  it to rna-seq of the  tumor macrophages and the genes that  were up  in the tumor associated macrophages and  the doctor macrophages correlate  really strongly whereas the genes up in  the stromal macrophages correlate  with the mammary tissue macrophages  and then when we look by flow cytometry  we can see the duster macrophages  here the cd11c positive lysose low  during tumor genesis as the cancer grows  we get enrichment for these cells and  for the more they mirror the morphology  and positioning of the doctor  microphotos in the normal duct  within the cancer  so to summarize the mammary gland has a  population of  unique resident macrophages that monitor  the epithelium  they're bone marrow derived during  puberty but before that  they are embryonic derived they  promote epithelial remodeling by  clearing away the  dyeing milk producing cells during  involution  and they're really similar to breast  tumor macrophages  and we hope that this discovery will  help us to understand  better tumor macrophages  and help us to modulate those to improve  cancer treatment  and so these dr macrophages they have a  unique  markers compared to the strabal  macrophages and they have this  intraepithelial localization  during pregnancy they proliferate to  increase in number  so they cover the whole gland and all of  the alveoli in lactation  and then in involution they infiltrate  the lumen and clear away these  dying milk producing cells and then in  tumor genesis in breast cancer they  maintain this positioning  throughout the cancer  so i'd like to thank now uh especially  my  supervisors jane viveda and rios and  jeff linderman everyone else who was  involved  in this paper in the lab bupinda  francois  um gordon smythe and luke gandolfo did  the bioinformatics analysis  uh felicity jacklin and everyone else in  the lab and the  division i'd like to thank the imaging  facility  animal and facts facilities at the  welterweight hall institute  at the doherty institute especially  scott mueller who helped with the  intravital imaging  with the project collaborators at a star  in singapore for the ms4a3 experiments  tissue joiners and funding  and here's jane and anne with their 3d  glasses because i do want to  quickly show you some cool things you  can do with  imarus with this 3d visualization that  not a lot of people have seen  and i'd be happy to chat about this with  people  and you can check out some of these 3d  visualizations here to look at with the  cyan red 3d glasses  and that's it thank you very much for  watching and  hope to connect with you in the real  world
 

